<!--
  Easy Access Rules for Information Security, in the shape EASA actually
  publishes it.  Anonymised: every heading below is a structural label or a
  rule reference; all body text is written for this fixture.

  Two things about this layout defeat a parser that expects the Air Ops shape
  ('ANNEX III (Part-ORO)' at Heading1):

  - The document consolidates two regulations, so Heading1 is spent naming
    each of them and the annexes are headed one level down, at Heading2.
  - The part marker is bracketed and closes the heading after the annex
    title — '[PART-IS.AR]' — rather than following the numeral in
    parentheses.  The delegated regulation states a single annex and does not
    number it at all: 'ANNEX — ... [PART-IS.D.OR]'.

  Read with the level fixed at Heading1, no part is found, the document falls
  through to article parsing, and every rule in it lands in one 'ARTICLES'
  part that neither regulation can be said to state.

  ANNEX III and ANNEX IV below are the other trap: they are amendment
  annexes.  They name parts of *other* regulations mid-heading and state
  none of their own, so a rule that reads a part marker anywhere in the
  heading invents a Part-ARA, a Part-ORA and a Part-21 annex that the
  Information Security regulations do not contain.
-->
<w:document xmlns:w="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/wordprocessingml/2006/main">
  <w:body>
    <w:p>
      <w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="Title"/></w:pPr>
      <w:r><w:t>Easy Access Rules for Information Security</w:t></w:r>
    </w:p>
    <w:p>
      <w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="Heading1"/></w:pPr>
      <w:r><w:t>Table of contents</w:t></w:r>
    </w:p>
    <w:p>
      <w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val="TOC3"/></w:pPr>
      <w:r><w:t>IS.AR.200 Information security management system</w:t></w:r>
